* ===========================================================================
*  A small preemptive multitasking monitor in MOL80 (a throwaway study, after
*  .kiro/specs/cpt8000-emulator/8080_multitasking_monitor_notes.md).
*
*  It runs on the emulated CPT machine with the 100 Hz task-switch timer
*  (RST 4, vector 0020H), so run it with:   cpt-run --timer monitor.bin
*  Output goes to the serial port.
*
*  TASK API
*    SPAWN     BC = the address of a subroutine.  It becomes a new task.  E = its quantum
*              in timer ticks (1..255, 10 ms each; 0 = the default, QUANTUM).  Returns
*              A = 0 and HL = the task's TCB (its handle), or A = 1 and HL = 0 if there is
*              no free TCB slot or no free stack.  The subroutine just RETurns when it is
*              done: its stack was primed with SYSEXIT underneath the entry point, so the
*              task is destroyed and its stack recycled automatically.
*    SETQUANT  HL = a task's TCB, A = its new quantum (0 = default).  Takes effect the next
*              time that task is dispatched.  A quantum is a time-slice WEIGHT, not a
*              priority: a task with quantum 10 gets twice the CPU of one with 5 when both
*              are always runnable, but never preempts it.  Blocking or YIELDing early gives
*              the rest of the quantum away.  Clobbers A, DE, HL.
*    YIELD     give up the rest of this quantum.
*    WAITSEM   A = semaphore number.  Blocks until it is acquired; waiters are served in
*              ring order starting after the signalling task, so none is starved.
*    SIGNALSEM A = semaphore number.  Releases it, handing it to the next waiter.
*              (WAITSEM/SIGNALSEM preserve BC and DE; SPAWN and the print routines do not.)
*    Monitor calls assume the caller runs with interrupts ENABLED and return with them
*    enabled (the 8080 cannot read its interrupt flag, so they cannot restore a prior DI).
*    The one exception is start-up, which begins with interrupts off and turns them on at
*    the first SPAWN.
*    A launcher hook: store a subroutine address in SPAWNREQ (0C0AH), and its quantum in
*    SPAWNQ (0C0CH), and the idle task will SPAWN it and clear SPAWNREQ.
*
*  STACK ALLOCATION
*    Stacks come from a pool of NSTK fixed-size blocks (STKSIZE bytes each) chained into a
*    free list: the first word of each free block points to the next free block.  Taking
*    and returning a block is a few instructions at the list head, done with interrupts
*    off.  A block is returned only AFTER the scheduler has switched away from it: the
*    exiting task parks its TCB in ZOMBIE, and the next SCHED reclaims the stack once the
*    CPU is running on another one.  Fixed blocks mean no fragmentation and O(1) alloc/free.
*    (Variable-size stacks would need a heap with coalescing; not worth it here.)
*
*  KEEPING INTERRUPTS ENABLED
*    Interrupts are off only where the data really needs to be consistent:
*      - the context switch itself (the CPU disables them on an interrupt anyway),
*      - popping/pushing the stack free list (a handful of instructions),
*      - testing and claiming one TCB slot (per slot, not the whole scan),
*      - testing/updating one semaphore.
*    SPAWN builds the new task's whole stack frame with interrupts ON: the slot is claimed
*    with status NEW (not runnable) and only becomes READY with a final single-byte store,
*    which is atomic.  Printing, delays and all task code run with interrupts on.
*    Fast path of a tick that does not end the quantum is about 120 cycles.
*
*  TCB layout (16 bytes):  +0 STATUS   +1 wait-for semaphore number
*                          +2 NEXT (word)   +4 saved SP (word)
*                          +6 stack block address (word)   +8 entry point (spawn scratch)
*                          +10 quantum, in ticks (byte)
*  STATUS: 0 free, 1 ready, 2 blocked on a semaphore, 3 new (being built), 4 zombie.
*
*  Simplifications vs. the notes: the TCB ring is static (4 slots, pre-linked).  Slot 0 is
*  the boot context, which becomes the idle task and is always READY.
*
*  Memory:  0000 reset jump      0020 timer vector (RST 4)       0100 code
*           0800 TCB table       0C00 variables                  0C10 semaphore table
*           0E00 TASKE (a fixed address, so a test can launch it)
*           1000-11FF stack pool (4 x 128)                       7F00 idle/boot stack
* ===========================================================================
*  Serial console (placeholder ports, as in cpm/bios.mol)
SERDATA   EQU 112
SERSTAT   EQU SERDATA+1
TXRDY     EQU 2
*
QUANTUM   EQU 5
NTASK     EQU 4
*  TCB table
TCB0      EQU 0800H
TCB1      EQU TCB0+16
TCB2      EQU TCB0+32
TCB3      EQU TCB0+48
*  Stack pool: NSTK blocks of STKSIZE bytes.  A new task's frame is 12 bytes at the top.
NSTK      EQU 4
STKPOOL   EQU 1000H
POOLEND   EQU STKPOOL+512
*  Semaphores: 2 bytes each (value 1 = free / 0 = held, waiter count), numbered from 0
SEMBASE   EQU 0C10H
PRTSEM    EQU 0
*  Variables (direct memory; VAR is our convention, see requirements)
LOCKFLAG  VAR 0C00H
QCOUNT    VAR 0C01H
CURRENT   VAR 0C02H
TICKS     VAR 0C04H
FREESTK   VAR 0C06H
ZOMBIE    VAR 0C08H
SPAWNREQ  VAR 0C0AH
SPAWNQ    VAR 0C0CH
*
*  ---- reset and interrupt vectors ------------------------------------------
          ORG 0
          /START
          ORG 20H
          /TICK
*
          ORG 100H
*  ---- start-up (runs once; then becomes the idle task) ---------------------
START     32512:HL
          HL:SP
          BANNER:HL
          CALL(PRINT)
          CALL(INITSTK)
          TCB0:HL
          HL:CURRENT
          QUANTUM:A
          A:QCOUNT
*  A gets a double-length quantum (10 ticks = 100 ms), B a normal one, C the default.
          TASKA:BC
          10:E
          CALL(SPAWN)
          TASKB:BC
          5:E
          CALL(SPAWN)
          TASKC:BC
          0:E
          CALL(SPAWN)
*  All four TCB slots are now in use: a fourth SPAWN must fail with A = 1.
          TASKD:BC
          0:E
          CALL(SPAWN)
          A=1? (
          FULLMSG:HL
          CALL(PRTLINE)
          )
*
*  The idle task: launch anything left in the mailbox, otherwise give the CPU away (HLT
*  wakes on the next interrupt).
IDLE      1:FI
          SPAWNREQ:HL
          H:A
          A-*L:A
          FZ=1? /IDLE2
          H:B
          L:C
          SPAWNQ:A
          A:E
          0:HL
          HL:SPAWNREQ
          CALL(SPAWN)
IDLE2     WAIT
          CALL(YIELD)
          /IDLE
*
*  ---- the timer interrupt: the preemptive context switch -------------------
*  Entered by RST 4 with interrupts disabled; the interrupted PC is on the task's stack.
TICK      A:STK
          BC:STK
          DE:STK
          HL:STK
          TICKS:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL:TICKS
          QCOUNT:A
          A-1:A
          A:QCOUNT
          FZ=1? /SWITCH
*  Quantum not used up: resume the same task.
          STK:HL
          STK:DE
          STK:BC
          STK:A
          1:FI
          RTN
*
*  YIELD: give up the rest of this task's quantum.  Same frame as the interrupt path.
YIELD     0:FI
          A:STK
          BC:STK
          DE:STK
          HL:STK
*  fall into SWITCH
*
*  SWITCH: the task's context is on its stack.  Save SP in its TCB, then schedule.
SWITCH    0:HL
          HL+SP:HL
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          CURRENT:HL
          4:BC
          HL+BC:HL
          E:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          D:(HL)
*  fall into SCHED
*
*  SCHED: choose the next READY task after CURRENT (round-robin), run it.  The idle task
*  is always READY, so this always finds one.  Blocked, new and zombie tasks are skipped.
SCHED     CURRENT:HL
SCNEXT    2:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          (HL):E
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):D
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          (HL):A
          A=1? /SCGO
          /SCNEXT
SCGO      HL:CURRENT
*  C = this task's quantum (BC is overwritten when its context is popped below).
          10:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          (HL):C
          CURRENT:HL
          4:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          (HL):E
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):D
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          HL:SP
*  Now running on the new task's stack: safe to recycle an exited task's stack.
          CALL(REAP)
          C:A
          A:QCOUNT
          STK:HL
          STK:DE
          STK:BC
          STK:A
          1:FI
          RTN
*
*  ---- SYSEXIT: reached when a task RETurns from its top level --------------
*  Park the TCB as a zombie and schedule; the stack is reclaimed by REAP once the CPU has
*  left it.
SYSEXIT   0:FI
          CURRENT:HL
          4:A
          A:(HL)
          HL:ZOMBIE
          /SCHED
*
*  REAP: if a task exited, return its stack block to the pool and free its TCB slot.
*  Runs inside SCHED (interrupts already off), on the next task's stack.
REAP      ZOMBIE:HL
          H:A
          A-*L:A
          FZ=1? RTN
          HL:STK
          6:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          (HL):E
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):D
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          CALL(STKFREE)
          STK:HL
          0:A
          A:(HL)
          0:HL
          HL:ZOMBIE
          RTN
*
*  ---- the stack pool ---------------------------------------------------------
*  INITSTK: chain all NSTK blocks into the free list (start-up only).
INITSTK   0:HL
          HL:FREESTK
          POOLEND:DE
          NSTK:B
ISLOOP    0FF80H:HL
          HL+DE:HL
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          FREESTK:HL
          L:A
          A:(DE)
          DE+1:DE
          H:A
          A:(DE)
          DE-1:DE
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          HL:FREESTK
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          B-1:B
          FZ=0? /ISLOOP
          RTN
*
*  STKALLOC: take a block off the free list.  Returns HL = block, or HL = 0 if none.
*  The caller must have interrupts off.
STKALLOC  FREESTK:HL
          H:A
          A-*L:A
          FZ=1? RTN
          (HL):E
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):D
          HL-1:HL
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          HL:FREESTK
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          RTN
*
*  STKFREE: push the block at HL back on the free list.  The caller must have interrupts
*  off.
STKFREE   DE:HL/HL:DE
          FREESTK:HL
          L:A
          A:(DE)
          DE+1:DE
          H:A
          A:(DE)
          DE-1:DE
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          HL:FREESTK
          RTN
*
*  ---- SPAWN: BC = the subroutine to run as a new task ---------------------------
*  Returns A = 0, HL = the TCB; or A = 1, HL = 0 (no free stack or no free TCB slot).
SPAWN     DE:STK
          BC:STK
          0:FI
          CALL(STKALLOC)
          1:FI
          H:A
          A-*L:A
          FZ=1? /SPNOSTK
          HL:STK
          CALL(TCBCLAIM)
          H:A
          A-*L:A
          FZ=1? /SPNOTCB
*  Record the stack block (+6), the entry point (+8) and the quantum (+10) in the claimed
*  TCB.  The stack now holds the TCB above the caller's E (the quantum, in C when popped).
          STK:DE
          STK:BC
          HL:STK
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          E:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          D:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          C:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          B:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          STK:DE
          STK:BC
          C:A
          0:FC<F>
          FZ=0? /SPQSET
          QUANTUM:C
SPQSET    C:(HL)
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          CALL(FRAMEBLD)
          0:A
          RTN
SPNOTCB   0:FI
          STK:HL
          CALL(STKFREE)
          1:FI
SPNOSTK   STK:BC
          STK:DE
          0:HL
          1:A
          RTN
*
*  SETQUANT: HL = a TCB, A = the task's new quantum in ticks (0 = the default).  A single
*  byte store, so it needs no DI.  Clobbers A, DE, HL.
SETQUANT  0:FC<F>
          FZ=0? /SQSET2
          QUANTUM:A
SQSET2    10:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          A:(HL)
          RTN
*
*  TCBCLAIM: find a free TCB slot and mark it NEW (3).  Returns HL = the slot, or 0.
*  Interrupts are off only for the test-and-set of each single slot.
TCBCLAIM  TCB0:HL
          NTASK:C
          16:DE
CLLOOP    0:FI
          (HL):A
          0:FC<F>
          FZ=0? /CLBUSY
          3:A
          A:(HL)
          1:FI
          RTN
CLBUSY    1:FI
          HL+DE:HL
          C-1:C
          FZ=0? /CLLOOP
          0:HL
          RTN
*
*  FRAMEBLD: HL = a NEW TCB whose +6 holds its stack block and +8 its entry point.  Primes
*  the stack exactly as a context switch would have left it:
*      SP -> L,H  E,D  C,B  flags,A   entry   SYSEXIT
*  then records SP and finally marks the task READY.  Runs with interrupts ENABLED: nothing
*  else can touch this TCB or stack until the last store makes the task runnable.
FRAMEBLD  HL:STK
          6:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          (HL):E
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):D
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):C
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):B
          116:HL
          HL+DE:HL
          HL:STK
          0:A
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          2:A
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          0:A
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          C:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          B:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          SYSEXIT:DE
          E:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          D:(HL)
          STK:DE
          STK:HL
          H:B
          L:C
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          HL+1:HL
          E:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          D:(HL)
          B:H
          C:L
          1:A
          A:(HL)
          RTN
*
*  ---- semaphores: A = the semaphore number ---------------------------------------
*  WAITSEM and SIGNALSEM preserve BC and DE (a task may keep a counter in B); they clobber
*  only A, HL and the flags.  The real work is in WAITSEM1 / SIGNALS1.
WAITSEM   BC:STK
          DE:STK
          CALL(WAITSEM1)
          STK:DE
          STK:BC
          RTN
*
SIGNALSEM BC:STK
          DE:STK
          CALL(SIGNALS1)
          STK:DE
          STK:BC
          RTN
*
*  SEMPTR: HL = the address of semaphore A (clobbers A, DE).
SEMPTR    A:E
          0:D
          SEMBASE:HL
          HL+DE:HL
          HL+DE:HL
          RTN
*
*  WAITSEM: acquire.  If free, take it.  Otherwise block (status 2, remembering which
*  semaphore) and switch away; SIGNALSEM wakes us already owning it (FIFO handoff).
WAITSEM1  0:FI
          A:B
          CALL(SEMPTR)
          (HL):A
          A=1? /WSACQ
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):A
          A+1:A
          A:(HL)
          CURRENT:HL
          2:A
          A:(HL)
          HL+1:HL
          B:(HL)
          CALL(YIELD)
          RTN
WSACQ     0:A
          A:(HL)
          1:FI
          RTN
*
*  SIGNALSEM: release.  With nobody waiting, mark it free.  Otherwise leave it held and
*  make the first task found blocked on it READY: ownership passes straight to that task.
SIGNALS1  0:FI
          A:B
          CALL(SEMPTR)
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):A
          0:FC<F>
          FZ=1? /SGFREE
          A-1:A
          A:(HL)
*  Scan the ring starting just AFTER the signalling task, so waiters are served in rotation
*  (a scan from slot 0 every time would let two tasks pass the lock between themselves
*  forever and starve the third).
          CURRENT:HL
          NTASK:C
SGLOOP    2:DE
          HL+DE:HL
          (HL):E
          HL+1:HL
          (HL):D
          DE:HL/HL:DE
          (HL):A
          A=2? /SGCHK
SGNEXT    C-1:C
          FZ=0? /SGLOOP
          B:A
          CALL(SEMPTR)
          1:A
          A:(HL)
          1:FI
          RTN
SGCHK     HL+1:HL
          (HL):A
          HL-1:HL
          A=B? /SGWAKE
          /SGNEXT
SGWAKE    1:A
          A:(HL)
          1:FI
          RTN
SGFREE    HL-1:HL
          1:A
          A:(HL)
          1:FI
          RTN
*
*  ---- output ----------------------------------------------------------------
*  PUTCH: send the character in C (waits for the transmitter; preemptible).
PUTCH     PORT(SERSTAT):A
          A+*TXRDY:A
          FZ=1? /PUTCH
          C:A
          A:PORT(SERDATA)
          RTN
*
*  BURN: waste about 3600 cycles, so a line takes long enough to span a time slice and the
*  semaphore is actually tested.
BURN      150:DE
BURNL     DE-1:DE
          D:A
          A-*E:A
          FZ=0? /BURNL
          RTN
*
*  PRINT: send the NUL-terminated string at HL, slowly.
PRINT     (HL):A
          0:FC<F>
          FZ=1? RTN
          A:C
          CALL(PUTCH)
          CALL(BURN)
          HL+1:HL
          /PRINT
*
*  PRTLINE: print the string at HL as one uninterrupted line, under semaphore PRTSEM.
*  LOCKFLAG (0 = off) lets a test switch the lock off to show what it prevents.
PRTLINE   HL:STK
          LOCKFLAG:A
          0:FC<F>
          FZ=1? /PLRAW
          PRTSEM:A
          CALL(WAITSEM)
          STK:HL
          CALL(PRINT)
          PRTSEM:A
          /SIGNALSEM
PLRAW     STK:HL
          /PRINT
*
*  ---- the demo tasks -----------------------------------------------------------
TASKA     MSGA:HL
          CALL(PRTLINE)
          /TASKA
*
TASKB     MSGB:HL
          CALL(PRTLINE)
          /TASKB
*
*  TASKC prints three lines and then simply RETurns: SYSEXIT frees its slot and stack.
TASKC     3:B
TCLOOP    MSGC:HL
          CALL(PRTLINE)
          B-1:B
          FZ=0? /TCLOOP
          RTN
*
TASKD     RTN
*
*  ---- data ---------------------------------------------------------------------
BANNER    DB "MOL80 monitor: 3 tasks", 13, 10, 0
FULLMSG   DB "task pool full", 13, 10, 0
MSGA      DB "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA", 13, 10, 0
MSGB      DB "BBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBBB", 13, 10, 0
MSGC      DB "CCCCCCCCCC", 13, 10, 0
MSGE      DB "EEEEEEEEEE", 13, 10, 0
*
*  The TCB ring: slot 0 (the idle/boot context) is ready; slots 1..3 start free.
*  Per slot:  status, wait-id | NEXT | saved SP | stack block | entry | quantum | spare
          ORG 0800H
          DB 1, 0
          DW TCB1, 0, 0, 0, QUANTUM, 0, 0
          DB 0, 0
          DW TCB2, 0, 0, 0, QUANTUM, 0, 0
          DB 0, 0
          DW TCB3, 0, 0, 0, QUANTUM, 0, 0
          DB 0, 0
          DW TCB0, 0, 0, 0, QUANTUM, 0, 0
*
*  Variable defaults: the print lock is on.
          ORG 0C00H
          DB 1
*  Semaphore 0 (the print lock) starts free: value 1, no waiters.
          ORG 0C10H
          DB 1, 0
*
*  TASKE lives at a fixed address (0E00H) so a test can launch it through SPAWNREQ.
*  It raises its own quantum to 20 ticks with SETQUANT, prints two lines and returns.
          ORG 0E00H
TASKE     CURRENT:HL
          20:A
          CALL(SETQUANT)
          2:B
TELOOP    MSGE:HL
          CALL(PRTLINE)
          B-1:B
          FZ=0? /TELOOP
          RTN
